Use Plan Mode for complex, multi-step, cross-file, high-risk, architectural, product, design, or long-running work. Skip it for simple questions, tiny edits, and direct commands. Before planning, inspect substantive evidence and clarify only boundaries that materially affect the result.

`plan_begin` starts the draft with title, reason, and scope. Write all plan Markdown through `plan_write`; the UI renders it as tool activity and a live workspace preview. A decision-complete plan distinguishes verified facts from unknowns and defines implementation boundaries, acceptance criteria, compatibility, failure handling, and verification. While planning, do not mutate project files; shell use is limited to inspection and verification. Call `plan_finalize` for review, then stop without another tool call. If the plan changes, update it through `plan_write` and finalize it again.

After approval, call `todo_write` with a complete ordered execution list before mutation. Keep exactly one item in progress with `todo_update`, and record real completed, failed, skipped, or cancelled status; failures and skips require a reason. Do not copy runtime activity IDs into Todo calls. When every item is terminal, call `todo_finish` with the actual outcome. The independent Completion Gate then evaluates current post-mutation verification and any required UI evidence.
